{"id":"404b4662-4a6b-418b-9157-310a210e76f0","object":"chat.completion","created":1785008736,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    # Build maps\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n['parent']\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\\n\\n    removed_ids = set()\\n    removal_order = []\\n\\n    # Sort by depth descending, then by id ascending (string)\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        # Skip if not removable, is root, or symbol is required\\n        if not node.get('removable', False):\\n            continue\\n        if node['parent'] is None:  # root\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Check if any child is retained (not removed yet)\\n        has_retained_child = any(\\n            child_id not in removed_ids for child_id in children.get(nid, [])\\n        )\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Simulate removal: check required symbols still present\\n        # Compute set of symbols from nodes that are not removed (including this node currently)\\n        # But we are about to remove 'nid', so exclude it.\\n        # We can just check for each required symbol if there exists some node not removed and not nid\\n        # that has that symbol.\\n        all_ok = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            found = False\\n            for n in nodes:\\n                if n['id'] == nid:\\n                    continue\\n                if n['id'] in removed_ids:\\n                    continue\\n                if n['symbol'] == sym:\\n                    found = True\\n                    break\\n            if not found:\\n                all_ok = False\\n                break\\n\\n        if all_ok:\\n            removed_ids.add(nid)\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n\\n    # Retained IDs: all IDs not removed, sorted\\n    retained_ids = sorted(\\n        [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed_ids]\\n    )\\n\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removal_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\\n    }\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` for task SE-PE-01. The task is about grammar-guided node reduction for a parse tree. Based on the fixture payload, we have a list of nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols. The output should have removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nThe description: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" But there is no explicit grammar or interestingness oracle given. However, from the fixture, we can infer: nodes have a parent-child tree structure. The root has parent null. Nodes have removable flag. We need to remove nodes that are removable, but must preserve required_symbols. Also cannot remove root or nodes with retained children. The algorithm: consider deeper nodes first, then shallower. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented. Use node ID as tie break at equal depth. Return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order.\n\nWe need to produce removed_node_ids (presumably in the order they were removed) and retained_node_ids (sorted). The case contains a list of nodes, and we need to decide which to remove. Based on the algorithm: iterate nodes sorted by depth descending, tie-breaking by node id (string, presumably lexicographic). For each node, if it is removable, check if removing it would still keep all required symbols represented in the remaining tree. Also, cannot remove if any of its children are retained (i.e., not removed). The condition \"never remove ... a node with retained children\" – we interpret as: if after processing, any child is retained, then parent cannot be removed. But since we are processing top-down? Actually deeper nodes first, so children are processed before parents? Deeper nodes have larger depth, so they are considered first. So when we consider a node at depth d, its children (depth d+1) have already been considered. So we can check if any child is retained (i.e., not removed). If it has retained children, we cannot remove it. Also we must ensure required symbols are still present: after removal, all required symbols must be present somewhere in the remaining tree. Also root is not removable.\n\nWe need to implement that.\n\nAdditionally, the description mentions \"grammar_valid\" and \"interestingness_preserved\" but we don't have grammar or interestingness function; the fixture seems to already have removable flag and required_symbols. So we just use that.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- Parse the case: case is a dict with 'nodes' list and 'required_symbols' list.\n- Build a mapping from node id to node object.\n- Build adjacency children mapping (list of child ids for each parent).\n- Determine initial set of retained nodes: all nodes that are not removed. Start with all nodes (since we haven't removed any). But we will iterate and possibly mark as removed.\n- We need to decide order of removal. The algorithm: deeper nodes first, then shallower, tie-break by node id. So sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending (string). For each node, check if it is removable (node['removable'] is True). Also it must not be root (parent is null). Also check if it has any retained children: we need to know at the moment of consideration which children are still retained. Since we process deeper nodes first, children will have been considered already. If a child is retained (i.e., not yet removed), then we should not remove the parent. So we can maintain a set of removed_ids. Initially empty. As we decide to remove a node, we add it to removed_ids. When checking a node, we see if any child is in retained set (i.e., not in removed_ids). If yes, skip.\n- Also for required symbols: before removing, simulate removal: check if after removing this node, all required symbols are still present in the tree. We need to check if any node that is not removed (including this one, but we are thinking of removing it) has each required symbol. So we need to know the set of symbols that would remain. We can compute: for each required symbol, check if there exists any retained node (excluding this node if we remove it) with that symbol. If all symbols can be satisfied by other nodes, then we can proceed.\n- Also, must not remove nodes that are required symbol? The description says \"never remove ... a required symbol\". So if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, we should not remove it, even if removable flag is True? The fixture shows n1 has removable false, but n2 has removable true and symbol s2, but required_symbols are root and s1. So n2 is not required. So we should also check: if node['symbol'] is in required_symbols, then do not remove. However the requirement \"never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children\" implies we should not remove a node that itself is a required symbol. So add that condition.\n- The root also has symbol 'root' which is required, so it's covered.\n\n- After processing all nodes, we have removed_ids. Then compute retained_ids = list of all ids not in removed_ids, sorted (ascending, presumably as strings).\n- removed_node_ids should be the order in which nodes were removed. Since we process in descending depth order, that's the removal order. But careful: we might skip nodes that are not removable; only when we actually remove we add to removed_ids in that order. So we maintain a list removal_order and append the id when we decide to remove.\n\nReturn {'removed_node_ids': removal_order, 'retained_node_ids': sorted_retained_ids}.\n\nEdge cases: nodes might be missing parent? Assume valid tree. Also depth might not be consistent but we can rely on given depth.\n\nImplementation note: We'll write a function solve(case) that returns a dict.\n\nNow produce the JSON with \"implementation\" key containing the Python source as a string.\n\nWe need to ensure escape newlines etc. The source will be a single string.\n\nWrite code carefully.\n\nLet's produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2054,"completion_tokens":1860,"total_tokens":3914,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1283},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":6},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}